// Editor-only settings. The package has no build step and no TypeScript:
// nothing here is read by node, by the tests or by the published tarball, and
// no dependency was added for it. Open the repository in VS Code (or any
// language server that reads jsconfig.json) and the JSDoc in src/ becomes
// navigable and checkable.
//
// checkJs covers the Node half of the plugin only: those modules are plain ESM
// with JSDoc types and reach the host through small dependency objects, so a
// single project can describe them without a build step.
//
// What is left out, and why:
//   - lib/client.js is the browser bundle. It is concatenated from the
//     src/client fragments, uses window/document/React and is loaded both
//     by the DSH panel and by the detached window shim; no Node project can
//     describe that environment, and checking it would bury the real errors.
//   - src/detached/ holds CommonJS Electron entry points. With "module":
//     "nodenext" the package "type": "module" would classify them as ESM and
//     every one of them would fail for the wrong reason.
//   - test/ runs under node:test, and node_modules/ is not ours.
//
// Globals such as process, Buffer and setTimeout come from the editor's Node
// typings. @types/node is deliberately not a dependency of this package; with
// none installed, expect unresolved-global warnings and nothing else.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "target": "es2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false
  },
  "include": [
    "src/host/**/*.js",
    "src/domain/**/*.js",
    "src/store/**/*.js",
    "src/sources/**/*.js",
    "src/paths.js",
    "src/http.js",
    "src/constants.js"
  ],
  "exclude": ["lib/client.js", "src/detached", "test", "node_modules"]
}
